From 8bf8d0790de53dd81422b1f8db9ce79945990a52 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:36:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Frame packing on nvidia-drm through dumb buffers
 without GBM

Issue #33, RTX 5090 with 1.2.2: in an HDMI 3D frame-packed mode the
driver refused the window's buffer ("its memory is not video memory"),
NVIDIA's GBM refused a linear buffer for scanout and rendering, and the
driver's dumb buffers, which 1.2.2 made next, never reached the driver:
NVIDIA's GBM refused to import them ("GBM could not import the dumb
buffer (Invalid argument)"). The 3D video went out in another layout.

The dumb buffers now take no part of GBM. Each is made on Kodi's DRM
file, its framebuffer is made of its handle with DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR
(which nvidia-drm asks of a frame-packed one), and it is mapped for the
processor. Kodi's queue holds buffers of GBM, so a small buffer of GBM
stands for each dumb one there; its user data is the dumb buffer's
framebuffer (drm_fb::own, DRMFramebuffer.h), which DrmFbGetFromBo()
gives first, and what the framebuffer is made of is asked of it, never
of the small buffer (FramePackedBufferUsable(),
DescribeFramePackedBuffer(), DrmOpaqueFbGetFromBo()).

nvidia-drm makes a dumb buffer pitch linear, of the SCANOUT kind, in
video memory on a card that has it, and fails it rather than place it
in system memory (nv_drm_dumb_create(), AllocateMemory()); such a
framebuffer has an NVKMS surface (nv_drm_framebuffer_init()).

Where NVIDIA's EGL imports a dumb buffer (its dma-buf), the GPU flips
each frame into it, and before use the processor reads, through the
mapping, that the GPU wrote a probe frame where the display engine
reads it at the driver's pitch (DumbLayoutProbe()). Otherwise, or where
that check fails, each frame is read back and copied by the processor,
the same bytes. The check of that copy looked for the probe pixels in
the first row of the buffer, where a right copy leaves black: they are
in its last row (RowHolds()). The order stays: the window's buffer,
then Kodi's own; kodi.log says which went out and how.
